 site from Europe, dating to 3499 BCE. It holds notable significance in its field. Holds notable historical significance, documented across 43 language editions of Wikipedia. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "Brú na Bóinne, located in County Meath, Ireland, is a prehistoric archaeological site that dates back to approximately 3499 BCE, marking it as one of the most significant Neolithic complexes in Europe. It encompasses a range of monumental structures, with the most notable being the passage tombs of Newgrange, Knowth, and Dowth, which reflect sophisticated burial practices and cosmological beliefs of early agrarian societies. This site is crucial for understanding the social and religious dynamics of early human communities in Northern Europe, serving as a testament to their architectural ingenuity and cultural expression.

The construction of the passage tombs at Brú na Bóinne demonstrates advanced engineering techniques, as seen in the precise alignment of Newgrange with the winter solstice sunrise. Archaeological excavations have revealed a wealth of artifacts, including pottery, stone tools, and human remains, suggesting that Brú na Bóinne served not only as a burial site but also as a ceremonial center for the surrounding communities. The elaborate engravings and symbols found on the stones indicate a complex belief system that likely influenced subsequent generations, providing insights into the spiritual life of Neolithic peoples.

